course: arts
lesson: 13
title: Colours: mixing, the colour wheel, warm and cool
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: With paint, a few colours can make hundreds: painters usually start from three primary colours, red, yellow and blue, and mixing two gives the secondary colours, green, orange and purple.
explain: Each paint soaks up part of the light, so mixing too many paints together soaks up almost everything and gives mud; picture Mia stirring her whole palette and getting puddle-water brown. Mixing all colours of LIGHT is different and makes white.
explain: Laid out in a circle, the colours make a colour wheel: reds, oranges and yellows are called warm, like fire, and blues, greens and purples cool, like ice; it is about mood, not real temperature, but it really changes how a picture feels.
explain: Colours opposite each other on the wheel, like orange and blue, make each other shout; adding white makes a tint, adding black makes a shade, and shadows are rarely black but darker, cooler colours.
explain: People react in their own words: "bruh" is a groan of disbelief, "fire" or "poppin'" means great and eye-catching, and an Irish "eejit" is an idiot.
fact: Mixing many paint colours together makes brown or grey, because each paint soaks up different parts of the light.
fact: Painters usually use red, yellow and blue as primary colours: blue and yellow make green, red and yellow make orange, red and blue make purple.
fact: Printers use cyan, magenta and yellow instead, which mix cleaner colours.
fact: Reds, oranges and yellows are called warm colours and blues, greens and purples cool colours, because of the feelings people link with them.
fact: Red-green colour blindness is common, affecting about 1 in 12 men and about 1 in 200 women of European descent; most colour-blind people still see many colours.
fact: Shadows usually have colour in them, and outdoor shadows, such as on snow, often look blue or purple; the French Impressionists of the 1870s became famous for painting blue and purple shadows.
fact: In China red is lucky and used at New Year and weddings; in many Western countries red can mean stop or danger, and white is worn at weddings.
fact: Real light can be much brighter than any paint, so painters make a sky look bright by putting darker colours beside it; this difference is called contrast.
fact: Complementary colours are opposite each other on the colour wheel, like orange and blue or red and green; side by side they make each other look stronger.
fact: Adding white to a colour makes a lighter tint; adding black makes a darker shade, and many painters darken with dark blue or brown instead so colours stay rich.
fact: For centuries purple dye was made from sea snails and was extremely expensive, so it became a sign of emperors; cheap purple dyes were invented only in the 1850s.
fact: Ultramarine blue, made from a stone, once cost more than gold.
exercise: Mia mixes every paint together. What does she get? | Muddy brown or grey | A bright rainbow colour | Pure white | Clear see-through paint
exercise: Daniel needs green but has no green paint. What should he mix? | Blue and yellow | Red and blue | Red and yellow | Black and white
exercise: What do red and blue make? | Purple | Green | Orange | Brown-yellow
exercise: Which three are the painter's usual primary colours? | Red, yellow and blue | Green, orange and purple | Black, white and grey | Pink, brown and shiny gold
exercise: Isla says the sea looks cold and the sunset warm. Is that silly? | No, warm and cool colours really change a picture's mood | Yes, colours have nothing to do with our feelings | Yes, blue is really the warmest colour | No, the paint itself gets hot or cold
exercise: Arjun's grandad calls the red jumper "brown". What is likely? | He has red-green colour blindness, which is common | He sees only black and white, like a really old film | He is joking, because colour blindness is not a real thing at all | He just needs to look at the jumper for much longer
exercise: About how many men have red-green colour blindness? | About 1 in 12 | About 1 in every 2 | About 1 in 1,000 | Nobody at all, ever
exercise: Lily paints a shadow black and it looks like a hole. What should she do? | Mix a darker, cooler colour of the things around | Use even more black paint | Leave shadows out of the picture | Paint the shadow bright white instead
exercise: Wei says red is lucky; Harry says red means danger. Who is right? | Both, colour meanings come from culture | Only Wei, because red means luck everywhere on Earth | Only Harry, red means danger | Neither, because colours have no meanings anywhere at all
exercise: Why does "sky blue" paint never look as bright as the real sky? | Real light is brighter than paint, so painters use contrast | The paint factory used the wrong blue | The sky is really grey, and paint shows it | Paint gets darker and darker every year it sits in the pot
exercise: Tyler's orange next to blue really pops. Why? | They are complementary, opposite on the colour wheel | Bright colours always clash side by side | Orange and blue are the same colour | Any two colours look the same together
exercise: Grace adds white to red and gets pink. What is that called? | A tint | A shade | A primary | A pure hue
exercise: Grace adds black to red. What has she made? | A shade | A pale tint | A secondary colour | A complementary colour
exercise: Why was purple "for kings"? | Its dye came from sea snails and cost a fortune | Kings invented the colour purple long ago | It was the only colour kings could see | Purple was banned for everyone else by nature
exercise: When were cheap purple dyes invented? | In the 1850s | In ancient Rome | In the year 2000 | Never, it was always cheap
exercise: Instead of red, yellow and blue, which colours do printers usually use? | Cyan, magenta and yellow | Red, green and blue | Black, white and grey | Gold, silver and bright bronze
test: Your mate goes, "mix all the colours and you get white, obviously." What is the truth? | With paint you get mud; only light mixes to white | He's right, all paint mixes to white in the end | All paint mixes to black, always, whatever you use | Mixing colours is impossible
test: Isla's brother says, "Colours don't have temperatures, ya eejit." Who is right? | Both partly: paint isn't hot, but warm and cool colours change the mood | Only the brother, because colours have no feeling | Only Isla, because blue paint is actually cold to touch and red paint is warm | Neither, because all colours feel the same to everyone, whatever they look like
test: What would happen if Daniel mixed red, yellow, blue and green all together hoping for something new? | He would most likely get a muddy brown | He would get a bright pink | He would get pure white, like light | He would get a rainbow of stripes on his paper
test: A cartoon shows a colour-blind character who sees the world in black and white. What is wrong? | Most colour-blind people just mix up certain colours | Nothing, that is how all colour blindness works | Colour-blind people see extra colours that others miss | Colour blindness doesn't exist
test: A football kit is orange and blue and everyone says it's "fire" (great). What makes it stand out? | Complementary colours make each other stronger | The colours are the same, so they blend | Orange is a cool colour | Blue is a warm colour, just like orange
test: Harry asks, "So which is it, mate, luck or danger?" What is the best answer? | It depends on the culture and the situation | Red always means luck, everywhere | Red always means danger | Red has no meaning, it is just a colour
test: You want a dark rich blue for night without it going flat. What could you try? | Darken with a deep blue or brown instead of only black | Add lots and lots of white until it gets darker | Add yellow to make it darker | Leave it exactly as it is and hope it dries darker overnight
test: Nan says her old book called purple "for kings". What would have happened if purple dye had been cheap back then? | Lots of ordinary people could have worn purple | Kings would have worn even more purple than before | Purple would have vanished | Nothing would change about who wore it or how much it cost
test: Impressionist paintings show blue and purple shadows on snow. Is that a mistake? | No, outdoor shadows often look blue or purple | Yes, shadows are always black, everywhere | Yes, snow has no shadows at all | No, because the painters were all colour-blind and saw wrong
test: Ruby's painted sky looks dull. How could she make it look brighter? | Put darker colours beside it for contrast | Add black paint into the sky to brighten it | Paint the land the same colour as the sky | Make the whole painting lighter
test: Mia's friend says "Bruh, it's puddle water." What is Mia's best next try? | Mix only two or three colours at a time | Mix even more colours together next time | Add every colour twice | Throw away all her paints and give up for good
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the admixture of many paints yields a brown or grey colour. | Each paint absorbs a different portion of the light, so little colour remains | Paint is by its nature brown in every instance, whatever its apparent hue | The mixture of all paints produces white light | The colours cancel one another and become transparent upon the surface
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning red-green colour blindness. | It is common, affecting approximately one man in twelve of European descent | It renders the affected person unable to perceive any colour whatsoever | It affects men and women in exactly equal measure in every population studied | It is unknown to medical science
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises complementary colours? | Colours situated opposite upon the colour wheel which intensify one another | Colours situated adjacent upon the colour wheel which blend together | Colours which invariably clash and must not be combined | Colours obtained by the addition of white
exam: Ascertain the reason for which purple was historically associated with persons of high rank. | Its dye, obtained from sea snails, was exceedingly costly | It was the sole colour visible to monarchs and their courts | It was devised by an emperor for his exclusive use | It occurred naturally only in royal gardens
